From: <author>
Subject: [PATCH] apfsck: data-recovery mode + catalog dumper (macOS build)

Recovery-mode changes to apfsck for reading damaged APFS containers from raw
disk images.  Based on upstream commit b9c4cb4 of https://github.com/linux-apfs/apfsprogs
(GNU GPL-2.0 — this patch is a derivative work and must remain GPL-2.0).
All recovery
behavior is opt-in via environment variables; with none set, apfsck behaves
as upstream.

Knobs:
  APFS_SOFT=1              report() prints problems but does not exit
  APFS_RECOVERY=1          lenient key/inode/object checks
  APFS_ALLOW_TRUNCATED=1   clamp s_block_count to the actual device size
  APFS_SB_BLOCK=<block>    read container NXSB from block N (dec or 0x hex)
                           instead of the standard location; use when the
                           primary superblock was overwritten
  APFS_DUMP=<file>         after parsing the volume snapshot, stream the
                           catalog B-tree to <file> as TSV and exit(0),
                           skipping the remaining fsck passes
  APFS_DUMP_XID=<xid>      dump a specific snapshot xid instead of the newest

Dump output columns (tab-separated):
  I <cnid> <mode> <uncompressed_size> <btime> <mtime>
  D <parent_cnid> <child_cnid> <drec_flags> <name(escaped: \\t \\n \\r \\\\ \\uXXXX)>
  E <cnid> <logical_offset_bytes> <length_bytes> <flags> <phys_block_num>
      (logical offset and length are BYTES; phys_block_num is an APFS block
       number — multiply by the block size for the image byte offset. The E
       records are the authoritative source for file sizes: inode
       uncompressed_size is often 0 on recovered images.)

compat/: small macOS-only shim. mmap() of sparse image regions fails with
EINVAL on macOS; mmap_shim.h replaces mmap/munmap with calloc+pread for the
apfsck read path only. WARNING: because pages are local copies, a binary
built with this shim must never be used to write to the image.

diff --git a/apfsck/apfsck.c b/apfsck/apfsck.c
index d26d255..17dbefb 100644
--- a/apfsck/apfsck.c
+++ b/apfsck/apfsck.c
@@ -18,6 +18,7 @@ int fd_tier2 = -1;
 unsigned int options;
 static bool weird_state;
 static char *progname;
+bool apfs_recovery_mode;	/* set from APFS_RECOVERY: lenient parsing for data recovery */
 
 /**
  * usage - Print usage information and exit
@@ -52,13 +53,17 @@ __attribute__((noreturn)) void system_error(void)
 }
 
 /**
- * report - Report the issue discovered and exit
+ * report - Report the issue discovered and exit (or continue if lenient)
  * @context: structure where corruption was found (can be NULL)
  * @message: format string with a short explanation
+ *
+ * If the environment variable APFS_SOFT is set, the report is printed but
+ * execution continues instead of exiting.  This makes it possible to run a
+ * full read-only pass over a badly corrupted filesystem and salvage whatever
+ * parses successfully.
  */
-__attribute__((noreturn, format(printf, 2, 3)))	void report(const char *context,
-							    const char *message,
-							    ...)
+void report(const char *context,
+	    const char *message, ...)
 {
 	char buf[128];
 	va_list args;
@@ -72,6 +77,10 @@ __attribute__((noreturn, format(printf, 2, 3)))	void report(const char *context,
 	else
 		printf("%s\n", buf);
 
+	if (getenv("APFS_SOFT")) {
+		fflush(stdout);
+		return;
+	}
 	exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
 }
 
@@ -125,6 +134,7 @@ int main(int argc, char *argv[])
 	char *filename;
 
 	progname = argv[0];
+	apfs_recovery_mode = getenv("APFS_RECOVERY") != NULL;
 	while (1) {
 		int opt = getopt(argc, argv, "cuvwF:");
 
